Modifications that change behaviour or interoperability, not just names:

- Service URI scheme is `popopx:` instead of the upstream scheme
  (src/Popopx/Messaging/ServiceScheme.hs).
- Key-derivation label strings were changed to `POPOPX*` - `POPOPXSbChainInit`,
  `POPOPXSbChain`, `POPOPXX3DH`, `POPOPXVerifyCode`, `POPOPXRootRatchet`,
  `POPOPXChainRatchet`, `POPOPXContactLink`, `POPOPXInvLink`, `POPOPXService` -
  and the BBS badge header to `POPOPX badges v1`. These derivations do NOT
  interoperate with upstream servers or agents, and data produced under the
  upstream labels cannot be decrypted by this version, or the reverse.
- That non-interoperability is the product of a decision, not an oversight, and it
  moved twice on 2026-11-17: `25d7213d` introduced these `POPOPX` spellings,
  `cb9e1aa3` restored the upstream spellings because at that point interoperating with
  upstream relays and released clients was still treated as a requirement, and this
  commit restores the `POPOPX` spellings under the ruling that POPOPX is a new system
  that is not interoperable with SimpleX and is served by servers compiled from this
  repository. Nothing had to be migrated for that ruling to be safe and it was checked
  rather than assumed: the client application is unreleased, no deployment exists, and
  the only stored value any of these derivations produces is inside an agent database
  or an already-issued link, of which there are none. `protocol/crypto-registry.md` is
  authoritative for each derivation's inputs and outputs, and its policy line now
  agrees with its own table: that file names these strings 14 times on 13 lines - 11
  distinct values, every one of them spelled `POPOPX` and none spelled `SimpleX`. Ten
  of the eleven are computed here: `git grep -o '"POPOPX[^"]*"' -- src
  ':(exclude)src/Popopx/FileTransfer' | wc -l` gives 10 occurrences and the same 10
  distinct values, the two occurrences left out by that exclusion being `--version`
  labels rather than KDF inputs. The eleventh, the call media key, has no call site in
  this repository at all - it is derived by the client application for a call
  negotiated at `callVersion` 3 or above, and the client keeps the upstream spelling of
  that one info string for calls negotiated below version 3, so a version-2 call and a
  version-3 call derive different media keys from the same ratchet material.
- Prometheus metric names are `popopx_smp_*`.
- Preset server addresses are this fork's own cluster, published as dual-stack
  addresses: a public hostname and a V3 .onion host for every relay. The
  published set is fifteen relays - eight SMP (`smp1..8.popopx.im`, no port,
  because 5223 is the protocol default), one chat relay (`relay1.popopx.im`,
  `:5227`), three file relays (`xftp1/6/8.popopx.im`, `:8443`) and three
  notification relays (`ntf1..3.popopx.im`, `:5226`); in each published address
  the port is written once, after the .onion host, and it applies to both hosts.
  Those are the ports scripts/ubuntu/deploy_ubuntu22.sh configures per role.
  Two of the four groups
  have a home in this repository: the nine SMP and chat-relay host-to-onion
  pairs in src/Popopx/Messaging/Agent/Protocol.hs (`extraSMPServerHosts`),
  where `git grep -o '\.popopx\.im' --
  src/Popopx/Messaging/Agent/Protocol.hs | wc -l` gives 9, and the three keyed
  file relays in src/Popopx/FileTransfer/Client/Presets.hs, repeated as the web
  list so that `git grep -o -E '@xftp[168]\.popopx\.im' -- src xftp-web | wc -l`
  gives 6. `extraSMPServerHosts` carries hosts and no keys, because the key is
  already in the address it completes: it appends the .onion host to an address
  that carries only the public one, which is why `relay1` belongs there even
  though no port ever does.
  The web list is xftp-web/web/servers.json - the `popopx` member, imported by
  xftp-web/vite.config.ts as `presets` and read there as `presets.popopx` -
  together with its build product
  apps/xftp-server/static/xftp-web-bundle/index.js. It differs from the Haskell
  preset in two ways, both forced by how a browser resolves these addresses:
  the parser reads only the first host and defaults it to 443
  (xftp-web/src/protocol/address.ts, and `parseHost` in vite.config.ts for the
  CSP origins), so the public host carries `:8443` explicitly, because port 443
  on these relays is Caddy's information site rather than the file protocol;
  and the .onion hosts are left out, because a browser with no Tor SOCKS proxy
  cannot reach them and the CSP would then name an origin the page can never
  use. That list is regenerated, not hand-edited: `cd xftp-web && npm run
  build:prod`, then the bundle's three files (`index.js`, `index.css`,
  `crypto.worker.js`) are replaced from `dist-web/assets/` - the
  `__vite-browser-external*.js` stubs that build also emits are not part of the
  committed bundle, and were not added by this change.
  Two sets of upstream addresses are kept on purpose. The m20220811 migration
  keeps both of its `ntf2.simplex.im` strings, because its `WHERE` clause has to
  match what an older database really stored, and rewriting the value it selects
  would leave the statement matching nothing. And four contact links that a
  server of this distribution serves keep upstream's addresses whole, in
  apps/common/Web/static/{index,link}.html and
  apps/xftp-server/static/file.html: each carries upstream's key, queue id and
  ratchet together with `smp6.simplex.im` or `smp17.simplex.im`, so moving only
  the hostname would aim the link at a relay of ours for a queue it does not
  hold. Those four move with a contact created on this fork's own relay, not
  with an edit of the host; the link domain on those same lines is already this
  fork's own (`popopx.chat`, and `popopx:` for the app-scheme link in
  link.html), so the relay address inside the fragment is the only upstream
  part left.
  The Flux list in servers.json is kept for a third
  reason: those servers belong to Flux, and taking over their hostname routes
  Flux's service under this fork's name.
  Re-check the residue with `git grep -o -i 'simplex\.im' -- . | grep -v
  '^NOTICE' | wc -l`, which gives 6 occurrences on 4 files: exactly the
  migration pair and the four contact links above. There is no SMP or
  notification preset carrying keys in this repository, and no STUN or TURN
  setting at all, so the other twelve published fingerprints have no home here -
  they belong to the client application, which is where they are consumed.
- Docker image organisation is `popopxchat`; installation paths are
  `/etc/popopx`, `/etc/popopx-xftp` and `/var/lib/popopx`, and the server
  management scripts are `popopx-servers-*`.
- The SQLite user-defined function `simplex_xor_md5_combine` is renamed
  `popopx_xor_md5_combine`, and the old name is not registered at all
  (src/Popopx/Messaging/Agent/Store/SQLite.hs). The four `rcv_queues` triggers that call it
  were rewritten in place in `Store/SQLite/Migrations/M20260411_service_certs.hs`, because
  SQLite resolves a trigger's functions when the statement is prepared: while a trigger names
  an unregistered function, every INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE on that table fails to compile.
  `Store/SQLite/Migrations/agent_schema.sql` records the same four triggers with the new name.
  No separate rename migration is kept: the chain cannot carry a step that leaves the schema
  unchanged, because `tests/AgentTests/SchemaDump.hs` requires each migration it rolls back to
  alter the schema dump. Rewriting a historical migration is safe only under this fork's
  premise that no database and no released client predates it. What the function computes is
  untouched, so hashes already stored stay correct and no wire format changes.
- 2026-11-23 - the release workflow changed so that an official tag publishes what the Ubuntu
  scripts consume. `prerelease:` is `false` in both release steps
  (`.github/workflows/build.yml:76`, `:459`): GitHub keeps a prerelease out of
  `/releases/latest`, which is the endpoint `install.sh:87` queries and the first one
